WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.681 --> 00:00:16.644
One fine June morning, Miss Polly Harrington hurried into her kitchen.

2
00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:21.045
Nancy, who was busy washing dishes, looked up in surprise.

3
00:00:22.286 --> 00:00:27.407
Although Nancy had worked for Miss Polly for only two months, she'd never seen her employer hurry.

4
00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:30.548
"Nancy," Miss Polly said.

5
00:00:32.372 --> 00:00:36.153
"Yes, ma'am," Nancy answered cheerfully as she continued drying a pitcher.

6
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:44.195
"When I'm speaking, you should stop working and listen," Miss Polly said in a stern voice.

7
00:00:45.596 --> 00:00:48.657
Nancy set the pitcher on the counter and almost tipped it over.

8
00:00:48.677 --> 00:00:51.237
"Yes, ma'am, I will.

9
00:00:52.578 --> 00:00:56.399
I only kept working because you said to hurry with the dishes this morning."

10
00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.840
Miss Polly frowned.

11
00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:02.877
"I didn't ask for explanations.

12
00:01:03.697 --> 00:01:05.098
I asked for your attention."

13
00:01:05.118 --> 00:01:07.299
"Yes, ma'am."

14
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.961
Nancy struggled not to sigh.

15
00:01:11.221 --> 00:01:14.923
This was Nancy's first job, and she wondered if there was any way to please Miss Polly.

16
00:01:16.164 --> 00:01:18.845
Nancy had needed to find work when her father died suddenly.

17
00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:24.308
Her sick mother was left with Nancy, who was 16, and 3 younger children.

18
00:01:26.604 --> 00:01:31.568
Before Nancy was hired, she only knew Miss Polly as one of the wealthiest residents of Beldingsville, Vermont.

19
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.374
Now Nancy knew her as a demanding woman who frowned if silverware clattered to the floor or doors

20
00:01:38.454 --> 00:01:44.199
banged. Miss Polly never smiled, even when forks and doors were silent.

21
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.622
And she never gave Nancy a word of praise.

22
00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:54.388
want you to clean up the little room in the attic," Miss Polly said.

23
00:01:55.428 --> 00:01:58.190
"After you remove the trunks and boxes, sweep the floor.

24
00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.631
Then set up the cot."

25
00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:03.674
"And where should I put the things I remove?"

26
00:02:03.694 --> 00:02:05.916
"In the front attic."

27
00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:08.097
Miss Polly paused for a moment.

28
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:15.442
"I suppose I should tell you that my niece, Miss Pollyanna Whittier, is coming to live with me.

29
00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:19.265
She's 11 years old, and that will be her room."

30
00:02:20.766 --> 00:02:23.228
Nancy's face broke into a wide grin.

31
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.141
"A little girl living here?

32
00:02:26.161 --> 00:02:28.582
Oh, that'll be nice!"

33
00:02:28.603 --> 00:02:34.266
"Nice? That isn't exactly the word I'd use."

34
00:02:34.666 --> 00:02:35.747
Miss Polly sniffed.

35
00:02:36.947 --> 00:02:39.929
"However, I intend to make the best of the situation.

36
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.411
I'm a good woman, and I know my duty."

37
00:02:45.332 --> 00:02:46.112
Nancy blushed.

38
00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.913
"Of course, ma'am.

39
00:02:48.354 --> 00:02:50.115
I wasn't saying that you weren't good.

40
00:02:51.816 --> 00:02:58.570
I mean I was only thinking that well a child might brighten things up for you."

41
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.092
"I don't see any need for that."

42
00:03:03.012 --> 00:03:05.153
Miss Polly abruptly turned to leave the kitchen.

43
00:03:05.173 --> 00:03:09.715
"Make sure you clean the corners of the room," she called over her shoulder.

44
00:03:09.735 --> 00:03:11.135
"Yes, ma'am."

45
00:03:14.357 --> 00:03:17.058
Nancy sighed and went back to drying dishes.

46
00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:22.140
A short time later, Miss Polly sat in her room, rereading a letter.

47
00:03:23.468 --> 00:03:27.389
When the letter had arrived from out West, it contained an unpleasant surprise.

48
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:34.971
Dear Miss Harrington, I regret to inform you that the Reverend John Whittier has died, making his daughter,

49
00:03:35.031 --> 00:03:36.212
Pollyanna, an orphan.

50
00:03:37.792 --> 00:03:40.393
A poor minister, he left nothing except a few books.

51
00:03:41.713 --> 00:03:44.714
I know that Reverend Whittier was your late sister's husband.

52
00:03:45.595 --> 00:03:50.096
Although your families didn't get along, he thought you might want to bring up Pollyanna, for your sister's

53
00:03:50.136 --> 00:03:56.275
sake. A local couple is traveling to Boston this week, so they can accompany Pollyanna for most of

54
00:03:56.315 --> 00:03:56.775
the journey.

55
00:03:58.016 --> 00:04:00.798
In Boston, they'll put Pollyanna on a train to Beldingsville.

56
00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:04.321
I'll let you know what train she is on.

57
00:04:05.742 --> 00:04:06.842
I hope to hear from you soon.

58
00:04:08.143 --> 00:04:15.328
Sincerely, Jeremiah White Miss Polly shook her head as she remembered her sister Jennie, Pollyanna's mother.

59
00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:20.492
When Jennie was twenty, she'd married Whittier against the wishes of her parents.

60
00:04:22.092 --> 00:04:27.394
They'd wanted her to marry a wealthy, older man, but she'd chosen the minister and moved far away.

61
00:04:29.275 --> 00:04:32.236
Miss Polly was 15 when her oldest sister married.

62
00:04:33.477 --> 00:04:36.598
For a while Jennie wrote to her family, although they never replied.

63
00:04:38.559 --> 00:04:40.780
All Jennie's babies died except Pollyanna.

64
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.242
Jennie named the baby for her two sisters, Polly and Anna.

65
00:04:48.563 --> 00:04:51.624
A few years later, Whittier wrote to say Jennie had died.

66
00:04:54.572 --> 00:04:57.114
Miss Polly was forty now and alone in the world.

67
00:04:58.615 --> 00:05:00.597
Her parents and two sisters had all died.

68
00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:06.142
Some people pitied her lonely life and urged her to have a friend live with her.

69
00:05:07.102 --> 00:05:10.866
But Miss Polly welcomed neither their sympathy nor their advice.

70
00:05:11.826 --> 00:05:15.489
She wasn't lonely; she liked being by herself and preferred quiet.

71
00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.832
Miss Polly stood up and put away the letter.

72
00:05:18.852 --> 00:05:23.316
"I'll do my duty and take in Jennie's daughter.

73
00:05:24.632 --> 00:05:28.727
But Pollyanna—what a ridiculous name!"
